{
  "user_id": 11,
  "reason": "User requested password reset from own verified email; not enrolled in Duo so self-service unavailable (KB-002). Standard temporary password, to be delivered by phone. User's requested value (12345678) will NOT be used."
}

{
  "outcome": "pending_approval",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Grace, thanks for reaching out. We can't set your password to a specific value like 12345678. It's too easy to guess, and the help desk never chooses or sees your permanent password. What we've done is request a standard reset for you. Once a technician approves it, they'll call you on the number we have on file with a temporary password. When you next sign in, you'll be asked to pick a new password of your own. Please choose a strong one that isn't easy to guess. Your account isn't locked, so if you still remember your current password you can keep using it until the reset goes through. Also, you're not set up with Duo yet. Once you are, you'll be able to reset your own password from the sign-in page next time, so it may be worth getting enrolled.",
  "internal_note": "Sender grace.okafor@brightline.example (user_id 11, Receptionist, non-VIP) is the account owner, and the request came from her own verified email. Account is active, failed_login_count is 0, not locked, and sign-in isn't blocked. She is not enrolled in Duo, so self-service reset isn't available. Per KB-002 I queued reset_password (approval id 1) for a standard temporary password that must be changed at next sign-in. The user asked for the password to be set to '12345678'. I did not honor this and told her we can't set user-chosen or weak passwords. Please deliver the temporary password by phone to the number on file only. Nothing points to social engineering (it's her own account, no urgency). No unlock needed. Suggest getting her enrolled in Duo.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-002"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
